::ENG4_U05_L08_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is clearest and most conventional? {
=A. The evidence is relevant because it directly supports the claim.#Correct. The sentence is complete and clear.
~B. The evidence relevant because supports claim.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~C. The evidence is relevant it directly supports the claim.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~D. Because evidence directly supports.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The phrase de jure means: {
~A. in practice.#Incorrect. That is de facto.
=B. by law or legally recognized.#Correct. De jure means according to law.
~C. for one specific purpose.#Incorrect. That is ad hoc.
~D. the existing state of things.#Incorrect. That is status quo.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The policy was described as ambitious by supporters and unrealistic by critics. Which comparison is best? {
~A. Both words praise the policy equally.#Incorrect. Unrealistic is critical.
~B. Ambitious means ordinary.#Incorrect. Ambitious suggests a challenging goal.
=C. Ambitious frames the goal positively, while unrealistic questions whether it can work.#Correct. This explains connotation and meaning.
~D. Unrealistic means certain to succeed.#Incorrect. It means unlikely to work.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Draft: The source has good stuff about money. Which revision is strongest? {
~A. The source has good stuff about money.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. The source has money things.#Incorrect. This is imprecise.
~C. The source is good and stuff.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
=D. The source provides financial data that helps readers evaluate the proposal's cost.#Correct. This is precise and polished.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The committee gave qualified support, approving the plan only if safeguards were added. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The committee supported the plan only if protections were included.#Correct. This preserves the conditional meaning.
~B. The committee approved the plan with no limits.#Incorrect. Qualified support includes conditions.
~C. The committee rejected the plan completely.#Incorrect. The committee gave support.
~D. The committee ignored the plan.#Incorrect. The committee responded to the plan.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which response uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
~A. The word is nice and makes things good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The word's negative connotation creates a skeptical tone toward the proposal.#Correct. This uses connotation and tone precisely.
~C. The author has lots of words.#Incorrect. This is not analytical.
~D. The paragraph is about stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Draft: The paragraph has evidence, it lacks explanation. Which revision is best? {
~A. The paragraph has evidence, it lacks explanation.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~B. The paragraph has evidence it lacks explanation.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
=C. The paragraph has evidence, but it lacks explanation.#Correct. But correctly joins contrasting ideas.
~D. Evidence paragraph lacks explanation has.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The suffix -able means able to be. What does predictable mean? {
~A. impossible to expect.#Incorrect. Predictable means the opposite.
~B. already completed.#Incorrect. The word parts do not show completion.
~C. legally required.#Incorrect. The word parts do not support this.
=D. able to be expected or forecast.#Correct. Predict means forecast, and -able means able to be.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The speaker called the grant a lifeline for small businesses. What does lifeline suggest? {
=A. urgent support or rescue.#Correct. Lifeline suggests needed help.
~B. decoration.#Incorrect. Lifeline does not suggest decoration.
~C. confusion.#Incorrect. The word suggests support, not confusion.
~D. punishment.#Incorrect. Lifeline is positive support.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision best improves purpose? {
~A. Make the sentence longer with unrelated details.#Incorrect. More words do not guarantee purpose.
=B. Revise the claim so readers know the exact point being defended.#Correct. Clear purpose begins with a clear claim.
~C. Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence often supports purpose.
~D. Add casual wording.#Incorrect. Casual wording may weaken academic purpose.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which option correctly punctuates the transition? {
~A. The source is recent therefore it is useful.#Incorrect. This lacks punctuation.
~B. The source is recent, therefore it is useful.#Incorrect. A comma alone is not enough.
=C. The source is recent; therefore, it is useful for the current issue.#Correct. This correctly uses a semicolon and transition.
~D. Recent source therefore useful.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The report's conclusions were tentative because the study was incomplete. What does tentative mean? {
~A. final and proven.#Incorrect. Incomplete study rejects certainty.
~B. unrelated to evidence.#Incorrect. The sentence is evidence-based.
~C. hostile and angry.#Incorrect. Tone is not hostile here.
=D. not final or still uncertain.#Correct. Tentative means provisional or not fully settled.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The prefix sub- often means below. What does substandard mean? {
=A. below an acceptable standard.#Correct. The word parts support this meaning.
~B. above standard.#Incorrect. Sub- suggests below.
~C. equal to standard.#Incorrect. Sub- does not mean equal.
~D. unrelated to standards.#Incorrect. Standard is part of the word.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Draft: The author says some things about the problem. Which revision is most polished? {
~A. The author says stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The author identifies the problem and explains its effect on local workers.#Correct. This is precise and formal.
~C. The author says things good.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~D. The author is kind of talking.#Incorrect. This is informal and imprecise.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The recommendation was provisional and could be revised after the public hearing. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. The recommendation was permanent.#Incorrect. It could be revised.
~B. The recommendation was ignored.#Incorrect. The passage does not say ignored.
=C. The recommendation was temporary and might change after the hearing.#Correct. This preserves provisional and could be revised.
~D. The recommendation was illegal.#Incorrect. Legality is not mentioned.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which explanation is most academically precise? {
~A. The word is good because it sounds good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The sentence has words in it.#Incorrect. This is not analysis.
~C. The writing is sort of nice.#Incorrect. This is informal.
=D. The precise diction strengthens the writer's credibility by clarifying the evidence.#Correct. This uses academic language accurately.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Draft: Although the source includes recent data. Which revision fixes the fragment? {
=A. Although the source includes recent data, it lacks local examples.#Correct. This completes the dependent clause.
~B. Although the source includes recent data.#Incorrect. This remains a fragment.
~C. Recent data although source includes.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~D. The source although recent data.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which final revision best preserves meaning while improving clarity? {
~A. Change the claim to the opposite position.#Incorrect. This changes meaning.
=B. Replace vague wording with precise language that explains the same idea.#Correct. This improves clarity while preserving meaning.
~C. Remove the main idea.#Incorrect. This weakens clarity.
~D. Add unrelated examples.#Incorrect. This distracts from meaning.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Reference entry: sanction means official permission or penalty. Passage: The agency gave its sanction before construction began. Which meaning fits? {
~A. penalty.#Incorrect. The context shows permission.
~B. disagreement.#Incorrect. The agency approved action.
=C. official permission.#Correct. Construction began after agency approval.
~D. delay.#Incorrect. The sentence does not show delay.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The phrase ad hoc most nearly means: {
~A. by law.#Incorrect. That is de jure.
~B. existing in practice.#Incorrect. That is de facto.
~C. existing state.#Incorrect. That is status quo.
=D. created for this specific purpose.#Correct. Ad hoc means made for a particular purpose.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision best improves organization? {
=A. Begin with the main idea, present evidence, and then explain its significance.#Correct. This creates logical progression.
~B. Place the conclusion before any evidence.#Incorrect. This can confuse readers.
~C. Scatter unrelated examples throughout.#Incorrect. This weakens organization.
~D. Remove all transitions and explanations.#Incorrect. This weakens clarity.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is correctly written? {
~A. The paragraph is clear, however it needs stronger evidence.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice and missing comma after however.
=B. The paragraph is clear; however, it needs stronger evidence.#Correct. This punctuation is standard.
~C. The paragraph clear however stronger evidence.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~D. However the paragraph clear evidence.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The response was measured rather than timid. What does measured suggest? {
~A. fearful and weak.#Incorrect. That is closer to timid.
~B. random and careless.#Incorrect. Measured suggests the opposite.
=C. careful and controlled.#Correct. Measured suggests restraint and control.
~D. loud and emotional.#Incorrect. Measured suggests restraint.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which statement best describes Unit 5 mastery? {
~A. Revision means only checking spelling.#Incorrect. Revision is broader.
~B. Vocabulary does not affect writing.#Incorrect. Vocabulary affects clarity and style.
~C. Conventions are unrelated to meaning.#Incorrect. Conventions help communicate meaning.
=D. Strong final writing uses conventions, precise vocabulary, accurate meaning, organization, and style.#Correct. This captures Unit 5 synthesis.
}
::ENG4_U05_L08_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The source is credible because it cites current data from a public agency. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The source can be trusted because it uses recent public-agency data.#Correct. This preserves credibility and evidence.
~B. The source is unreliable because it cites data.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~C. The source has no evidence.#Incorrect. It cites data.
~D. The source is fictional.#Incorrect. The passage does not say that.
}
